3: }\n164: ```\n165: \n166: ### Metadata Tagging\n167: \n168: Tag AI-generated files for future auditing. This helps track which code was generated vs hand-written, enabling targeted reviews during audits.\n169: \n170: ```ts\n171: // @generated - AI-assisted implementation\n172: // @audit-date - 2026-01-15\n173: // @generator - claude-opus-4-5\n174: ```\n175: \n176: ## The Rejection Protocol\n177: \n178: If the audit fails any check in the supreme checklist:\n179: \n180: ### Step 1: Stop\n181: \n182: Do not proceed with the commit, push, or report. A failed audit means the code is not ready.\n183: \n184: ### Step 2: Analyze\n185: \n186: Identify the specific deviation:\n187: \n188: - Which gate failed?\n189: - What is the root cause?\n190: - Is this a pattern issue or an isolated problem?\n191: \n192: ### Step 3: Remediate\n193: \n194: Apply the fix immediately. Do not defer:\n195: \n196: - Fix the type error, not suppress it\n197: - Add the missing test, not skip the check\n198: - Resolve the security issue, not add a TODO\n199: - Correct the pattern, not document the workaround\n200: \n201: ### Step 4: Re-Audit\n202: \n203: Restart the checklist from step 1. A partial re-run is not sufficient because fixes can introduce new issues.\n204: \n205: ## The Supreme Audit Checklist\n206: \n207: Before any AI-generated code is accepted:\n208: \n209: | Check                   | What to Verify                                              |\n210: | ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n211: | Contract integrity      | Code matches defined interfaces (Zod, TypeScript types)     |\n212: | Architectural alignment | Idiomatic patterns for the stack (not cargo-culted)         |\n213: | Security sanitization   | All inputs validated, no secrets in logs or code            |\n214: | Performance hygiene     | No N+1 queries, images optimized, bundle size within budget |\n215: | Cleanliness audit       | No `console.log`, debug artifacts, commented-out code       |\n216: | Traceability            | Non-obvious decisions documented with \"why\" comments        |\n217: | Test coverage           | New code has tests, existing tests still pass               |\n218: | Dependency audit        | No new high/critical vulnerabilities introduced             |\n219: \n220: ## Context-Aware Review\n221: \n222: Surface-level static analysis catches formatting and basic lint issues but misses deeper problems. Effective AI code review must also assess:\n223: \n224: - **Architectural fit** -- does the change align with the system's design direction?\n225: - **Requirement alignment** -- does the implementation match what was actually requested?\n226: - **Convention consistency** -- does the code follow the team's established patterns?\n227: - **Future maintainability** -- will this code be easy to modify in six months?\n228: - **Integration safety** -- does the change interact correctly with existing code?\n229: \n230: ## Quality Metrics for AI Code\n231: \n232: Track these metrics to measure the effectiveness of AI code review:\n233: \n234: | Metric                 | Target            | Why It Matters                       |\n235: | ---------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------------ |\n236: | Post-merge defect rate | <2% of AI-gen PRs | Catches issues the review missed     |\n237: | Review iteration count | <3 rounds per PR  | Measures AI output quality           |\n238: | Time from PR to merge  | <24 hours         | Measures review bottleneck           |\n239: | Gate pass rate         | >90% on first run | Measures code generation quality     |\n240: | Revert rate            | <1% of merged PRs | Measures overall process reliability |"}
{"step_index":660,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:06:59Z","input_tokens":7535,"cache_read_tokens":132360,"output_tokens":391,"thinking":"Examining the remaining files provides a deeper understanding of the quality audit process, potential anti-patterns, verification techniques, and self-evaluation methodologies. This should inform the assessment criteria and ensure comprehensive analysis across multiple facets, including application security and Rust-specific considerations.\n\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":150,"Path":"/srv/git/odbus-verified/.agents/skills/quality-auditor/references/audit-rubric.md","StartLine":1}},{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":150,"Path":"/srv/git/odbus-verified/.agents/skills/quality-auditor/references/anti-patterns-guide.md","StartLine":1}},{"name":"read_file","args":{"Path":"/srv/git/odbus-verified/.agents/skills/verification-before-completion/SKILL.md"}}]}
{"step_index":661,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:07:04Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:07:04Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:07:05Z\nShowing lines 1-150 of 302\n1: ---\n2: title: Audit Rubric\n3: description: Multi-dimension audit rubric with pass/warn/fail criteria, weighted scoring methodology, automated vs manual checklists, and audit report format\n4: tags:\n5:   [\n6:     audit-rubric,\n7:     pass-warn-fail,\n8:     weighted-scoring,\n9:     automated-checks,\n10:     manual-review,\n11:     scoring-methodology,\n12:   ]\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # Audit Rubric\n16: \n17: A structured rubric for executing quality audits with clear pass/warn/fail thresholds, weighted scoring, and a distinction between automated and manual audit items.\n18: \n19: ## Pass / Warn / Fail Thresholds\n20: \n21: Each dimension receives a 1-10 score, then maps to a threshold:\n22: \n23: | Threshold | Score Range | Meaning                                         | Action Required           |\n24: | --------- | ----------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n25: | Pass      | 7-10        | Meets or exceeds standards                      | No action                 |\n26: | Warn      | 5-6         | Below standard but functional                   | Improvement plan required |\n27: | Fail      | 1-4         | Critical gaps, blocks release or further work   | Immediate remediation     |\n28: | N/A       | --          | Dimension does not apply (e.g., no UI for a11y) | Redistribute weight       |\n29: \n30: **Hard fail conditions (override any score):**\n31: \n32: - Phase 0 resource completeness fails -- cap overall at 6.0\n33: - Any security dimension score of 3 or below -- flag as release blocker\n34: - Any critical anti-pattern found without a mitigation plan -- fail the audit\n35: \n36: ## Dimension Rubric Summary\n37: \n38: Each dimension uses consistent criteria across three levels. See `dimension-rubrics.md` for full per-dimension detail.\n39: \n40: ### Code Quality (10%)\n41: \n42: | Level | Criteria                                                      |\n43: | ----- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n44: | Pass  | Consistent patterns, low duplication, complexity under limits |\n45: | Warn  | Some code smells, moderate duplication, complexity hotspots   |\n46: | Fail  | No patterns, widespread duplication, god objects              |\n47: \n48: ### Architecture (10%)\n49: \n50: | Level | Criteria                                                        |\n51: | ----- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n52: | Pass  | Clear module boundaries, low coupling, scalable design          |\n53: | Warn  | Some coupling, unclear boundaries in non-critical areas         |\n54: | Fail  | Circular dependencies, tight coupling, no separation of concern |\n55: \n56: ### Documentation (10%)\n57: \n58: | Level | Criteria                                                   |\n59: | ----- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n60: | Pass  | All features documented, examples work, accurate to code   |\n61: | Warn  | Major features documented, some gaps, some stale examples  |\n62: | Fail  | Missing docs for critical paths, misleading or no examples |\n63: \n64: ### Usability (10%)\n65: \n66: | Level | Criteria                                                      |\n67: | ----- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n68: | Pass  | Under 10 minutes to first success, clear error messages       |\n69: | Warn  | 10-30 minutes to first success, some confusing error messages |\n70: | Fail  | Over 30 minutes to first success, cryptic errors, no defaults |\n71: \n72: ### Performance (8%)\n73: \n74: | Level | Criteria                                                              |\n75: | ----- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n76: | Pass  | Within performance budget, no N+1 queries, acceptable Core Web Vitals |\n77: | Warn  | Occasional slow paths, minor budget overruns, no profiling            |\n78: | Fail  | Unusable latency, resource exhaustion, no optimization                |\n79: \n80: ### Security (10%)\n81: \n82: | Level | Criteria                                                       |\n83: | ----- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n84: | Pass  | OWASP Top 10 addressed, inputs validated, no hardcoded secrets |\n85: | Warn  | Most inputs validated, minor dependency vulnerabilities        |\n86: | Fail  | Hardcoded secrets, SQL injection, missing auth on endpoints    |\n87: \n88: ### Testing (8%)\n89: \n90: | Level | Criteria                                                           |\n91: | ----- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n92: | Pass  | 80%+ coverage, unit/integration/e2e present, tests verify behavior |\n93: | Warn  | 60-79% coverage, mostly unit tests, some flaky tests               |\n94: | Fail  | Under 40% coverage, no integration tests, tests verify mocks       |\n95: \n96: ### Maintainability (8%)\n97: \n98: | Level | Criteria                                                     |\n99: | ----- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n100: | Pass  | Low tech debt, clear contribution path, semver compliance    |\n101: | Warn  | Moderate debt, some outdated deps, unclear contribution path |\n102: | Fail  | High debt, abandoned dependencies, no versioning strategy    |\n103: \n104: ### Developer Experience (10%)\n105: \n106: | Level | Criteria                                                    |\n107: | ----- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n108: | Pass  | Under 5 minutes setup, fast feedback loop, good error DX    |\n109: | Warn  | 5-15 minutes setup, some friction, basic error messages     |\n110: | Fail  | Over 15 minutes setup, broken tooling, no debugging support |\n111: \n112: ### Accessibility (8%)\n113: \n114: | Level | Criteria                                                    |\n115: | ----- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n116: | Pass  | WCAG 2.1 AA compliant, keyboard navigable, screen reader OK |\n117: | Warn  | Partial WCAG compliance, keyboard works for main flows      |\n118: | Fail  | No WCAG compliance, keyboard traps, inaccessible content    |\n119: \n120: ### CI/CD (5%)\n121: \n122: | Level | Criteria                                             |\n123: | ----- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n124: | Pass  | Automated build, test, deploy pipeline with rollback |\n125: | Warn  | Automated build and test, manual deploy              |\n126: | Fail  | No automation, manual build and deploy               |\n127: \n128: ### Innovation (3%)\n129: \n130: | Level | Criteria                                           |\n131: | ----- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n132: | Pass  | Novel approach or unique value proposition         |\n133: | Warn  | Conventional but competent implementation          |\n134: | Fail  | Derivative with no differentiation (rarely blocks) |\n135: \n136: ## Weighted Scoring Methodology\n137: \n138: ### Calculating the Overall Score\n139: \n140: Multiply each dimension score by its weight, then sum:\n141: \n142: ```text\n143: Overall = (CodeQuality * 0.10) + (Architecture * 0.10) + (Documentation * 0.10)\n144:         + (Usability * 0.10) + (Performance * 0.08) + (Security * 0.10)\n145:         + (Testing * 0.08) + (Maintainability * 0.08) + (DX * 0.10)\n146:         + (Accessibility * 0.08) + (CICD * 0.05) + (Innovation * 0.03)\n147: ```\n148: \n149: Round to one decimal place.\n150: "}
{"step_index":662,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:07:05Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:07:05Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:07:05Z\nShowing lines 1-150 of 185\n1: ---\n2: title: Anti-Patterns Guide\n3: description: Catalog of code, architecture, security, testing, and process anti-patterns to identify during quality audits with detection strategies\n4: tags:\n5:   [\n6:     anti-patterns,\n7:     code-smells,\n8:     security-vulnerabilities,\n9:     testing-antipatterns,\n10:     process-issues,\n11:   ]\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Anti-Patterns Guide\n15: \n16: A catalog of anti-patterns to identify during quality audits, organized by category. Each anti-pattern includes detection strategies and severity levels.\n17: \n18: ## Code Anti-Patterns\n19: \n20: | Anti-Pattern           | Signal                                   | Severity |\n21: | ---------------------- | ---------------------------------------- | -------- |\n22: | God objects            | Single class/module doing everything     | High     |\n23: | Spaghetti code         | No clear flow, tangled dependencies      | High     |\n24: | Copy-paste programming | Duplicated blocks with minor variations  | Medium   |\n25: | Magic numbers          | Unexplained literals in logic            | Medium   |\n26: | Global state abuse     | Mutable shared state without controls    | High     |\n27: | Deep nesting           | 4+ levels of indentation                 | Medium   |\n28: | Long methods           | Functions exceeding 50 lines             | Medium   |\n29: | Primitive obsession    | Using primitives instead of value types  | Low      |\n30: | Feature envy           | Method uses more of another class's data | Medium   |\n31: | Dead code              | Unreachable or unused code paths         | Low      |\n32: | Type bypassing         | `any` and `@ts-ignore` causing drift     | High     |\n33: | Debug artifacts        | `console.log` and test scaffolding left  | Medium   |\n34: \n35: ### Detection Strategies\n36: \n37: ```bash\n38: # Find functions over 50 lines (approximate)\n39: rg -c \"^}\" -g \"*.ts\" | sort -t: -k2 -rn | head -20\n40: \n41: # Find potential magic numbers\n42: rg \"\\b(?:100|200|404|500|1000|3600|86400)\\b\" -g \"*.ts\" --stats\n43: \n44: # Find duplicated string literals\n45: rg -o '\"[^\"]{10,}\"' -g \"*.ts\" --no-filename | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -10\n46: \n47: # Find deeply nested code\n48: rg \"^\\s{16,}\" -g \"*.ts\" -c | sort -t: -k2 -rn\n49: \n50: # Find type bypassing\n51: rg \"(: any|as any|@ts-ignore|@ts-expect-error)\" -g \"*.ts\" --stats\n52: \n53: # Find debug artifacts\n54: rg \"console\\.(log|debug|info|warn)\" -g \"*.ts\" -g \"!*.test.*\" -g \"!*.spec.*\"\n55: ```\n56: \n57: ## Architecture Anti-Patterns\n58: \n59: | Anti-Pattern          | Signal                                        | Severity |\n60: | --------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n61: | Tight coupling        | Changes cascade across modules                | High     |\n62: | Circular dependencies | A depends on B depends on A                   | High     |\n63: | Missing abstractions  | Implementation details leak across boundaries | Medium   |\n64: | Over-engineering      | Unnecessary complexity for simple problems    | Medium   |\n65: | Anemic domain model   | Data structures with no behavior              | Medium   |\n66: | Big ball of mud       | No discernible architecture pattern           | Critical |\n67: | Leaky abstraction     | Internal details exposed through interface    | Medium   |\n68: | Vendor lock-in        | Core logic depends on specific vendor APIs    | High     |\n69: | Paradigm mixing       | Inconsistent patterns confuse humans and AI   | Medium   |\n70: \n71: ### Detection Strategies\n72: \n73: ```bash\n74: # Find files importing from many different modules (high fan-out)\n75: rg -c \"^import\" -g \"*.ts\" | sort -t: -k2 -rn | head -20\n76: \n77: # Find circular imports (approximate -- look for mutual references)\n78: rg \"^import.*from\" -g \"*.ts\" --no-heading | sort > /tmp/imports.txt\n79: \n80: # Find barrel files that re-export everything (coupling amplifier)\n81: rg \"^export \\* from\" -g \"index.ts\" -g \"index.tsx\"\n82: ```\n83: \n84: ## Security Anti-Patterns\n85: \n86: | Anti-Pattern             | Signal                            | Severity |\n87: | ------------------------ | --------------------------------- | -------- |\n88: | Hardcoded secrets        | API keys, passwords in source     | Critical |\n89: | SQL injection            | Unsanitized user input in queries | Critical |\n90: | XSS vulnerabilities      | Unescaped output in templates     | High     |\n91: | Missing authentication   | Endpoints without auth checks     | Critical |\n92: | Insecure defaults        | Debug mode in production          | High     |\n93: | Excessive permissions    | Over-scoped API keys or roles     | Medium   |\n94: | Missing rate limiting    | Endpoints without throttling      | Medium   |\n95: | Logging sensitive data   | PII or secrets in log output      | High     |\n96: | Missing input validation | No schema validation on user data | High     |\n97: \n98: ### Detection Strategies\n99: \n100: ```bash\n101: # Find potential hardcoded secrets\n102: rg -i \"(api.?key|secret|password|token)\\s*[:=]\" -g \"*.ts\" -g \"!*.test.*\"\n103: \n104: # Find potential SQL injection\n105: rg \"query\\(.*\\$\\{\" -g \"*.ts\"\n106: rg \"execute\\(.*\\+\" -g \"*.ts\"\n107: \n108: # Find missing auth checks in API routes\n109: rg \"export (async )?function (GET|POST|PUT|DELETE)\" -g \"*/api/**/*.ts\" -l\n110: \n111: # Find potential XSS in React (dangerouslySetInnerHTML)\n112: rg \"dangerouslySetInnerHTML\" -g \"*.tsx\"\n113: \n114: # Check for sensitive data in logs\n115: rg \"console\\.\\w+\\(.*(?:password|secret|token|key)\" -g \"*.ts\" -i\n116: ```\n117: \n118: ## Testing Anti-Patterns\n119: \n120: | Anti-Pattern              | Signal                                          | Severity |\n121: | ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n122: | No tests                  | Zero coverage                                   | Critical |\n123: | Flaky tests               | Non-deterministic pass/fail                     | High     |\n124: | Test duplication          | Same scenario tested multiple ways              | Medium   |\n125: | Testing implementation    | Tests break on refactor without behavior change | High     |\n126: | Missing edge cases        | Only happy path tested                          | Medium   |\n127: | Slow tests                | Test suite takes >10 minutes                    | Medium   |\n128: | Test pollution            | Tests depend on execution order                 | High     |\n129: | Over-mocking              | Tests verify mocks, not behavior                | Medium   |\n130: | Snapshot abuse            | Snapshots used as primary assertion strategy    | Medium   |\n131: | Missing integration tests | Only unit tests, no integration coverage        | High     |\n132: \n133: ### Detection Strategies\n134: \n135: ```bash\n136: # Check test coverage\n137: npx vitest --coverage --reporter=json\n138: \n139: # Find tests without assertions\n140: rg \"it\\(|test\\(\" -A 20 -g \"*.test.*\" | rg -v \"expect\\(|assert\"\n141: \n142: # Find snapshot-heavy test files\n143: rg \"toMatchSnapshot|toMatchInlineSnapshot\" -c -g \"*.test.*\" | sort -t: -k2 -rn\n144: \n145: # Find tests with timeouts (flaky test signal)\n146: rg \"setTimeout|waitFor|sleep\" -g \"*.test.*\" -c | sort -t: -k2 -rn\n147: ```\n148: \n149: ## Process Anti-Patterns\n150: "}
{"step_index":663,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:07:05Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:07:05Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:07:05Z\nShowing lines 1-120 of 120\n1: ---\n2: name: verification-before-completion\n3: description: Use when about to claim work is complete, fixed, or passing, before committing or creating PRs - requires running verification commands and confirming output before making any success claims; evidence before assertions always\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # Verification Before Completion\n7: \n8: ## Overview\n9: \n10: **Core principle:** Evidence before claims, always.\n11: \n12: **Violating the letter of this rule is violating the spirit of this rule.**\n13: \n14: ## The Iron Law\n15: \n16: ```\n17: NO COMPLETION CLAIMS WITHOUT FRESH VERIFICATION EVIDENCE\n18: ```\n19: \n20: If you haven't run the verification command in this message, you cannot claim it passes.\n21: \n22: ## The Gate Function\n23: \n24: ```\n25: BEFORE claiming any status or expressing satisfaction:\n26: \n27: 1. IDENTIFY: What command proves this claim?\n28: 2. RUN: Execute the FULL command (fresh, complete)\n29: 3. READ: Full output, check exit code, count failures\n30: 4. VERIFY: Does output confirm the claim?\n31:    - If NO: State actual status with evidence\n32:    - If YES: State claim WITH evidence\n33: 5. ONLY THEN: Make the claim\n34: \n35: Skip any step = lying, not verifying\n36: ```\n37: \n38: ## Common Failures\n39: \n40: | Claim | Requires | Not Sufficient |\n41: |-------|----------|----------------|\n42: | Tests pass | Test command output: 0 failures | Previous run, \"should pass\" |\n43: | Linter clean | Linter output: 0 errors | Partial check, extrapolation |\n44: | Build succeeds | Build command: exit 0 | Linter passing, logs look good |\n45: | Bug fixed | Test original symptom: passes | Code changed, assumed fixed |\n46: | Regression test works | Red-green cycle verified | Test passes once |\n47: | Agent completed | VCS diff shows changes | Agent reports \"success\" |\n48: | Requirements met | Line-by-line checklist | Tests passing |\n49: \n50: ## Red Flags - STOP\n51: \n52: - Using \"should\", \"probably\", \"seems to\"\n53: - Expressing satisfaction before verification (\"Great!\", \"Perfect!\", \"Done!\", etc.)\n54: - About to commit/push/PR without verification\n55: - Trusting agent success reports\n56: - Relying on partial verification\n57: - Thinking \"just this once\"\n58: - Tired and wanting work over\n59: - **ANY wording implying success without having run verification**\n60: \n61: ## Rationalization Prevention\n62: \n63: | Excuse | Reality |\n64: |--------|---------|\n65: | \"Should work now\" | RUN the verification |\n66: | \"I'm confident\" | Confidence ≠ evidence |\n67: | \"Just this once\" | No exceptions |\n68: | \"Linter passed\" | Linter ≠ compiler |\n69: | \"Agent said success\" | Verify independently |\n70: | \"I'm tired\" | Exhaustion ≠ excuse |\n71: | \"Partial check is enough\" | Partial proves nothing |\n72: | \"Different words so rule doesn't apply\" | Spirit over letter |\n73: \n74: ## Key Patterns\n75: \n76: **Tests:**\n77: ```\n78: ✅ [Run test command] [See: 34/34 pass] \"All tests pass\"\n79: ❌ \"Should pass now\" / \"Looks correct\"\n80: ```\n81: \n82: **Regression tests (TDD Red-Green):**\n83: ```\n84: ✅ Write → Run (pass) → Revert fix → Run (MUST FAIL) → Restore → Run (pass)\n85: ❌ \"I've written a regression test\" (without red-green verification)\n86: ```\n87: \n88: **Build:**\n89: ```\n90: ✅ [Run build] [See: exit 0] \"Build passes\"\n91: ❌ \"Linter passed\" (linter doesn't check compilation)\n92: ```\n93: \n94: **Requirements:**\n95: ```\n96: ✅ Re-read plan → Create checklist → Verify each → Report gaps or completion\n97: ❌ \"Tests pass, phase complete\"\n98: ```\n99: \n100: **Agent delegation:**\n101: ```\n102: ✅ Agent reports success → Check VCS diff → Verify changes → Report actual state\n103: ❌ Trust agent report\n104: ```\n105: \n106: ## When To Apply\n107: \n108: **ALWAYS before:**\n109: - ANY variation of success/completion claims\n110: - ANY expression of satisfaction\n111: - ANY positive statement about work state\n112: - Committing, PR creation, task completion\n113: - Moving to next task\n114: - Delegating to agents\n115: \n116: **Rule applies to:**\n117: - Exact phrases\n118: - Paraphrases and synonyms\n119: - Implications of success\n120: - ANY communication suggesting completion/correctness"}
